## Why This Is Asked
Auditing and non-repudiation in CNF gateways are critical for multi-tenant environments. This question validates practical expertise with Kubernetes Secrets, HMAC signing, sidecar log pipelines, and retention configuration—essential skills for implementing post-deployment security controls.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped auditing and non-repudiation
- HMAC signing using per-tenant Kubernetes Secrets
- Sidecar-based log forwarding to in-cluster sinks
- ConfigMap-driven log retention and namespace scoping

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signLog(logLine, secret) {
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(logLine)
    .digest('hex');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests resilience‑oriented CNF design for multi‑tenant traffic, focusing on per‑tenant state, timing windows, and safe recovery

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant state tracking within a sidecar
- Rolling window counters and cooldown handling
- 503 responses during open state, then auto‑recover

## Code Example
```javascript
class Breaker {
  constructor() { this.map = new Map(); }
  allow(tenant) {
    const s = this.map.get(tenant) || {f:0, t:0, open:0};
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now < s.open) return false;
    if (now - s.t > 60000) { s.f = 0; s.t = now; }
    const ok = s.f < 5;
    if (!ok) s.open = now + 30000;
    this.map.set(tenant, s);
    return ok;
  }
  fail(tenant) {
    const s = this.map.get(tenant) || {f:0, t:Date.now(), open:0};
    s.f += 1; s.t = Date.now(); this.map.set(tenant, s);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to test the breaker under load? Probe metrics and simulate bursts
- How to persist breaker state across restarts? Serializable store
- How to tune thresholds for different tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical end-to-end config provenance and policy enforcement for multi-tenant CNF platforms, a common production risk area beyond image provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Config payload signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SBOM attachment for config changes
- Admission control with Kyverno/OPA enforcing signature, SBOM, and tenant labels
- Canary namespace validation before rollout
- Per-tenant rollback mechanisms and status surfacing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo workflow: verify config update integrity before applying
async function verifyConfigUpdate(payload, signature, sbom) {
  const okSig = await verifySignature(payload, signature);
  const okSBOM = await validateSBOM(payload, sbom);
  const tenantOk = checkTenantLabel(payload);
  return okSig && okSBOM && tenantOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback semantics across tenants?
- How would you handle SBOM drift when multiple tenants update concurrently?

## Why This Is Asked
The candidate demonstrates practical understanding of idempotence in multi-tenant CNFs via sidecar patterns and Redis TTLs.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotence and deduplication per tenant
- Redis SETNX with TTL for race-free dedupe
- Sidecar data flow and fallback handling

## Code Example
```go
// simplified sketch
package main
import "net/http"
func Handle(req *http.Request) {
  tenant := req.Header.Get("X-TENANT-ID")
  id := req.Header.Get("X-REQUEST-ID")
  key := tenant + ":" + id
  // pseudo Redis call with TTL 120s
  ok := redis.SetNX(key, "1", 120 * time.Second)
  if !ok {
    // respond 409 with {"error":"duplicate"}
    return
  }
  // forward to backend
}
```

